---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_DESCRIPTION, SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={SITE_TITLE} description={SITE_DESCRIPTION} />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<div class="development-philosophy-container">
				<div class="dp-hero">
					<h1>Development Philosophy</h1>
					<p>
						I approach engineering problems through a continuous 
						<strong> Why → What → How → Why </strong>cycle.
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="dp-cycle">
					<div class="dp-step">
						<h2>Why</h2>
						<p>
							Why does the problem exist?
							Before implementing a solution, I first identify the root cause,
							the actual business or technical problem, and the constraints
							around it.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="dp-step">
						<h2>What</h2>
						<p>
							What needs to be done to solve the problem?
							I evaluate possible approaches, trade-offs, and the impact of
							each decision before choosing what to build or change.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="dp-step">
						<h2>How</h2>
						<p>
							How should it be implemented, and how can we ensure it works?
							I consider architecture, implementation details, testing,
							observability, and operational requirements.
						</p>

						<ul>
							<li>Scalability</li>
							<li>Reliability</li>
							<li>Maintainability</li>
							<li>Observability</li>
							<li>Performance</li>
							<li>Operational complexity</li>
							<li>Long-term technical debt</li>
						</ul>
					</div>

					<div class="dp-step">
						<h2>Why — Again</h2>
						<p>
							Why did the system behave differently from what we expected?
							After implementation, I verify the result against the original
							problem and continuously investigate unexpected behavior.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="dp-iteration">
					<p>
						Unexpected behavior is not simply a failure — it is new information.
						I use it to revisit the <strong>Why</strong>, redefine the
						<strong> What</strong>, improve the <strong>How</strong>, and continue
						the cycle.
					</p>

					<p>
						<strong> Why → What → How → Why → What → How → ...</strong>
					</p>
				</div>

				<p class="dp-summary">
					This approach helps me avoid solving symptoms instead of root causes,
					while balancing technical quality, business requirements, and
					long-term system health.
				</p>
			</div>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
